00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:50.310
Hey, you know what?

293
00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:51.670
People in Texas say, Chunk It.

294
00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:55.410
Here I was being, like, all self-conscious because you said, no, it's Chunk It.

295
00:10:55.410 --> 00:11:00.070
Then I'm talking to my mom over the holidays, and we're talking about going through the garage,

296
00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:01.550
and she goes, ah, just Chunk It.

297
00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:03.150
It's a Texas thing, lady.

298
00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:03.730
It is.

299
00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:07.630
I will admit it because I came home that day, and I was telling my husband, Lee, who grew

300
00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:12.270
up in Texas, all about the Chunk It thing and how much it made me laugh.

301
00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:18.710
And he said to me, Jenna, I moved to Texas in middle school, and I had that same conversation.

302
00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:20.090
Someone said, just Chunk It.

303
00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:21.050
And I said, what?

304
00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:21.810
Chunk It?

305
00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:23.230
He's like, they say it.

306
00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:23.750
They do.

307
00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.110
It's like pop and soda.

308
00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:26.430
Thank you.

309
00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:26.750
Right?

310
00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:27.750
Thank you.

311
00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:28.510
And I guess-

312
00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:29.350
Sorry, Texas.

313
00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:30.750
Don't be sorry.

314
00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:31.930
Just Chunk It.

315
00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:36.770
And I guess in St. Louis, they say, I'm going to lie on the table.

316
00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:37.210
No.

317
00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:39.570
Lay out all your cards.

318
00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:40.850
Lay out all your cards.

319
00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:41.710
Shall we begin?

320
00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:43.110
Let's begin.

321
00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:43.710
All right.

322
00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:50.530
Performance Review, Season 2, Episode 8, written by Larry Wilmore and directed by Paul Feig.

323
00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:51.530
I'm going to give you a summary.

324
00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:56.490
In this episode, Michael is supposed to be conducting performance reviews of his staff,

325
00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:01.030
but instead, he calls everyone into his office one by one to help him decipher a voicemail

326
00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:01.590
from Jan.

327
00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:05.890
Because if you remember in the previous episode, he and Jan hooked up.

328
00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:06.510
They did.

329
00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:11.130
They smooched in the parking lot, maybe played some grabby games back at her place.

330
00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:11.550
Yeah.

331
00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:16.510
And he is convinced now that they are boyfriend and girlfriend, even though it's clear that

332
00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:19.090
Jan has said, this is a one-time thing.

333
00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:20.450
Please make nothing of it.

334
00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:22.910
Michael is determined to read between the lines.

335
00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:29.430
Also in this episode, Pam and Jim pranked Dwight by convincing him that it is Friday instead

336
00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:33.350
of Thursday, the end of the summary.

337
00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:34.490
Summary is done.

338
00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:37.190
Jenna, that was a fantastic summary.

339
00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:37.990
Thank you.

340
00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:38.890
Well done.

341
00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:39.810
Thank you so much.

342
00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:40.770
All right.

343
00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:41.890
You want fast fact number one?

344
00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:42.370
I do.

345
00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:45.990
Fast fact number one, I said, this is written by Larry Wilmore.

346
00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:52.390
You guys, I hope you remember, he played Mr. Brown in our Diversity Day episode.

347
00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:53.030
Yes.

348
00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:56.530
Larry was a writer and producer and an amazing performer.

349
00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:57.390
Yes.

350
00:12:57.570 --> 00:12:59.550
And we are going to speak with Larry today.

351
00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:00.910
Larry is so great.

352
00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:02.910
I have such fond memories of working with him.

353
00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:05.190
You know, he created the Bernie Mac show.

354
00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:07.770
He has been a correspondent on The Daily Show.

355
00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:11.230
We've talked a little bit about this super talented guy.

356
00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:16.710
I'm really excited to talk to him because he was just, he's so smart and funny.

357
00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:20.270
I think that was one of the things about our writer's room is like how freaking smart

358
00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:20.690
they were.

359
00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:21.990
They were really smart.

360
00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:26.230
I think people underestimate how smart you have to be to be a great writer.

361
00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:26.830
Yeah.

362
00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:28.370
Like they really go hand in hand.

363
00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:30.730
I think, I don't know.

364
00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.690
Sometimes I wonder, have we wasted all these minds?

365
00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.170
Should they be off making scientific discoveries?

366
00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:39.410
Because some of them were some just like the most brilliant people I've ever met.

367
00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:41.070
And they're just sitting writing great jokes.

368
00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:43.710
But then without them, we wouldn't have the fitness orb.

369
00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:46.810
Fast fact number two, I was really sick in this episode.

370
00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:48.530
You were so sick.

371
00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:49.230
Do you remember that?

372
00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:55.230
Not only do I remember that you were sick, but I wrote down a quote that I read online.

373
00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:56.130
It says-

374
00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:57.050
Where did you read this?

375
00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:00.470
On Wikipedia, on the Office Performance Review Wikipedia.

376
00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.210
It says, Jenna Fisher came down with a cold.

377
00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:09.890
Instead of postponing, however, Fisher memorized and performed all her lines, resulting in fellow

378
00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:13.290
cast member Angela Kinsey calling her a trooper.

379
00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:16.550
You were in this episode because you were really, really sick.

380
00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:21.230
But that's one thing I think you guys, I don't know if you guys know this, but when you're

381
00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:23.650
on a set and you get sick, you don't go home.

382
00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:24.070
No.

383
00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:28.390
They literally prop you up because so many people, like what, 300 people?

384
00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:30.730
Their whole day revolves around that day's filming.

385
00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:31.130
Yeah.

386
00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:32.310
And so it's-

387
00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:34.730
They can't shut down the filming just because you don't feel good.

388
00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:35.170
Yeah.

389
00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:38.930
So a doctor will show up, give you a shot, and you stay at work.

390
00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:39.510
Oh, yeah.

391
00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:42.510
I mean, I've seen them tape people.

392
00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.750
They're like, oh, have you stopped vomiting for 30 minutes?

393
00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:48.170
On set and see what we can grab.

394
00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:48.710
Oh, yeah.

395
00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:49.490
Like that happens.

396
00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:50.710
So I was really sick.

397
00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:54.910
This was my first time ever having to perform this ill.

398
00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:56.410
I took tons of Sudafed.

399
00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:58.330
I had a sinus infection.

400
00:14:58.670 --> 00:14:59.590
You were a little loopy.

401
00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:01.410
I was super loopy.

402
00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:03.770
Here's what's crazy.

403
00:15:04.390 --> 00:15:11.830
At the end of the week, Greg Daniels called my manager about my performance in this episode.

404
00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:13.870
And my manager called me.

405
00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:14.910
I get this message.

406
00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:15.990
Jenna, please call me.

407
00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:19.570
Greg Daniels has called about your performance in this week's episode, and I want to speak

408
00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:20.310
with you about it.

409
00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:21.690
Oh, shiitake mushrooMs.

410
00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:22.970
I thought I was being fired.

411
00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:23.330
Yeah.

412
00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:25.070
I thought, oh, my gosh.

413
00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:26.250
This is it.

414
00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:26.790
That's it.

415
00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:27.650
I don't know why.

416
00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:28.070
Did you?

417
00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:32.750
I, as an actor, especially in the early days and on early shows, I always felt like I

418
00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:33.870
could be fired at any moment.

419
00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:38.030
Oh, me and Brian and Oscar were always like, accounting's totally replaceable.

420
00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:39.830
We could be fired at any time.

421
00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:40.650
Well, that's not true.

422
00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:45.210
But yes, you have this feeling like I'm failing constantly.

423
00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:45.530
Yeah.

424
00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:51.250
So my manager says, Jenna, we got this phone call, and she got all my agents on the phone

425
00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:53.370
too, which you're like, oh, my gosh.

426
00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:56.430
They only do that if it's great news or horrible news.

427
00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:57.170
Or really bad news.

428
00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:57.490
Yeah.

429
00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:02.710
So I'm like, and she's like, we received a phone call from Greg Daniels because he wanted

430
00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:07.850
all of us to know how outstanding you were in this episode, that it was some of your

431
00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:13.350
best work, and to please say, whatever it is you did to prepare for this episode, keep

432
00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:13.730
it up.

433
00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:15.930
Take Sudafed.

434
00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:20.850
I was like, I don't know what to do with this compliment.

435
00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:26.330
Clearly, I'm a fantastic actress on Sudafed.

436
00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:26.750
Yeah.

437
00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:28.510
Well, okay.

438
00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:29.230
Okay.

439
00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:30.670
Should I move on to fast fact number three?

440
00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:31.010
Yes.

441
00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:31.390
All right.

442
00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:33.890
Fast fact number three is also a belated fast fact.

443
00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:38.630
I realized I missed something a couple of episodes back in the fight.

444
00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:39.090
What?

445
00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:43.530
Which applies to all the episodes moving forward, talking about cold opens.

446
00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:44.410
Oh.

447
00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.410
Do you want to tell folks what a cold open is?

448
00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:47.830
Yes.

449
00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:53.970
So in a script, scripts usually have like a three-act structure where you tell the story,

450
00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:54.970
right?

451
00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:56.590
And like a little mini three-act.

452
00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:56.890
Yeah.

453
00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:01.430
Well, we started doing this thing that we called the cold open, and it's before the

454
00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:06.890
credits, and it's a little joke or almost like a standalone moment that might apply later

455
00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:08.490
to the episode, but also might not.

456
00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:08.850
Might not.

457
00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:10.650
It's just like its own little moment.

458
00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:12.390
So that was something that our show did.

459
00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:16.690
We had these little cold opens, and then we're also going to start adding tags soon.

460
00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:17.710
Ooh, tags are fun.

461
00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:18.950
Tags were-

462
00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:19.750
At the end.

463
00:17:19.850 --> 00:17:20.510
At the very end.

464
00:17:20.590 --> 00:17:24.190
We would come back from a commercial break, and you'd get like a seven-second tag.

465
00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:25.090
They were very short.

466
00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:25.850
Really short.

467
00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:26.750
Another little joke.

468
00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:27.290
Yeah.

469
00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:30.830
They were always really short, like maybe just one page, three lines, just something

470
00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:31.450
really tiny.

471
00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:31.990
Yes.

472
00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:39.210
In this episode, we have a cold open that is one of our most famous cold opens, the Fitness

473
00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:39.810
Orb.

474
00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:40.510
Yes.

475
00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:46.830
Which is so great, and we had a ton of fan questions about this, probably our most frequently

476
00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:47.730
asked moment.

477
00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:49.570
About this episode, for sure.

478
00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:50.130
Definitely.

479
00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:57.770
For example, Fernanda Fuentes and Derek Ford both asked, how many times did you have to

480
00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.410
film the cold open with Dwight's fitness ball?

481
00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:05.690
And David Nicholas asked, how many fitness orbs did you go through before it popped just

482
00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:06.090
right?

483
00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:09.370
Well, everyone, it was not meant to pop.

484
00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:13.190
Oh, Jenna, you look really excited.

485
00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:14.710
Are you sitting on some info?

486
00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:16.310
I'm sitting on some info.

487
00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:19.030
I traded messages with Phil Shea.

488
00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:20.850
Phil Shea, Prop Master.

489
00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:21.790
Yes.

490
00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:24.870
He told me all about the fitness orbs.

491
00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:27.810
So first of all, he bought 17.

492
00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:29.950
17 fitness orbs.

493
00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:30.350
Yes.

494
00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:33.130
And he practiced with John using three of them.

495
00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:36.810
Phil Shea sat on them, and John, like, hit them.

496
00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:37.530
Yes.

497
00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.170
Phil Shea was such, talk about a trooper, Phil Shea.

498
00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:40.950
He's amazing.

499
00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:46.050
So the idea was that John would stick the scissors into the fitness orb, and it would

500
00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:47.450
deflate very slowly.

501
00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.830
And the camera would be in front of Dwight's desk, and you would just see him very slowly

502
00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:57.610
melt to the floor as Jim very smugly looks on.

503
00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.870
Yes, that was what was in the script.

504
00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:03.410
So they practiced three times.

505
00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:04.530
It worked perfectly.

506
00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.430
And they had 14 left for the scene.

507
00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:12.630
And Phil said to John, as long as you don't hit the seam of the ball with the scissors, which

508
00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:16.950
would be like a one in million chance that you could do it, the ball will slowly deflate,

509
00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:18.330
and it will work as planned.

510
00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:20.850
So it's also very hard to see the seam.

511
00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:21.730
Oh, yeah.

512
00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:22.030
Yeah.

513
00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:23.810
I mean, yeah.

514
00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:25.390
You would have to hit it just right.

515
00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:26.010
Yes.

516
00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:28.170
It would be basically impossible to do.

517
00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:34.110
So they did 13 takes, and it worked just right, and everyone was happy, and they were

518
00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:35.070
about to move on.

519
00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:38.330
And Phil said, I have one more ball.

520
00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:38.890
Yeah.

521
00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:41.510
So Paul Feek said, oh, what the heck?

522
00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:42.490
Let's just do one more.

523
00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:47.290
And in all the 13 takes, you guys, Rain, as Dwight, slowly sunk to the ground.

524
00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:48.170
Like, phew.

525
00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:48.590
Yeah.

526
00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:49.050
Right?

527
00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:49.830
And it was perfect.

528
00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:53.370
So they have an extra ball.

529
00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:54.790
They're like, we'll just do one more.

530
00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:55.310
Let's do it.

531
00:19:55.430 --> 00:19:58.790
On that last take, John hit the seam.

532
00:19:58.930 --> 00:19:59.630
By accident.

533
00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:03.990
And you can see the shock on Rain's face.

534
00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:05.170
It exploded.

535
00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:06.830
And he fell to the floor.

536
00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.950
We were all completely shocked.

537
00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:10.130
We were like, oh, my God.

538
00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:13.090
And you can totally see John break.

539
00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:14.570
You can see his shoulders.

540
00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:17.230
And he very quickly, oh, he's such a pro.

541
00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:19.710
He turns his back in his face to the camera.

542
00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:20.250
He, like, dives out of the scene.

543
00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:20.460
Yeah.

544
00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:21.450
He just, like, leaves.

545
00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:23.490
Phyllis was really surprised, too.

546
00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:26.950
I swear you can hear somebody say, what?

547
00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:31.870
Like, either John or Rain is like, what is happening?

548
00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:34.070
And they were going to cut that out.

549
00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:35.450
They were like, oh, my gosh.

550
00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:36.290
We're so sorry, Rain.

551
00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:36.990
Are you all right?

552
00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:38.370
We were laughing so hard.

553
00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:43.530
And when they went in to edit the episode, they put in what was scripted.

554
00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:45.490
They put in the slow deflate.

555
00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:52.450
And it was Larry Wilmore, the writer, who said, you have to put the blooper one in.

556
00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:54.150
You have to put the fast pop in.

557
00:20:54.230 --> 00:20:55.110
Yeah, he fought for that.

558
00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:56.010
He fought for that.

559
00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:56.950
And he was right.

560
00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:58.390
That is an amazing moment.

561
00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:02.270
That is an example of just a perfect cold open.

562
00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:04.350
And now, I think we should take a little break.

563
00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:06.150
And we'll come back with our interview with Larry.

564
00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:09.650
Yes, we're going to come back and talk to Larry Wilmore, writer of this episode.

565
00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:19.150
All right, Sam.

566
00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:20.590
Let's give Larry a call.

567
00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:21.930
Yeah, let's call him up.

568
00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:28.170
Hey, how's it going?

569
00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:29.330
Hi, Larry.

570
00:21:29.710 --> 00:21:31.210
It's so nice to hear you guys.

571
00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:32.010
Same.

572
00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:32.690
Oh, my gosh.

573
00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:33.190
Same.

574
00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:35.110
We're so happy this worked out.

575
00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:36.510
Congrats on the pod, guys.

576
00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:37.750
What a great idea, too.

577
00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:39.030
Oh, thank you.

578
00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:39.310
Thank you.

579
00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:40.210
We're loving it.

580
00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:46.130
I mean, you know, Larry, we're just two chatty BFFs that get to talk about our favorite

581
00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:46.830
job ever.

582
00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:48.250
From the beginning.

583
00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:49.390
From the beginning.

584
00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:50.030
Okay.

585
00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:52.030
Well, Larry, thank you so much for joining us.

586
00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:55.030
Do you know you are our first writer as a guest?

587
00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:55.730
Wow.

588
00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:56.390
Woo-hoo.

589
00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:57.110
Woo-hoo.

590
00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:03.790
And so one of the questions we're asking everyone that comes on is, how did it come about that

591
00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:05.130
you became part of The Office?

592
00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:07.790
God, I have so many fond memories of that time.

593
00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:10.590
Mine is a little, a bit more complicated, I guess, than everybody else.

594
00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:14.190
I had just started a deal with NBC at that time.

595
00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:17.310
I had just done the Bernie Mac show, and I knew Greg from before.

596
00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:20.550
I had done a show called The PJs, and he was on King of the Hill, and we used to see

597
00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:24.370
each other all the time, and Greg was a buddy of mine, and he had never done a single

598
00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:28.830
camera show before, and NBC wanted to know if I wanted to consult on that while I was

599
00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:31.850
developing, you know, just to be another voice in there.

600
00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:34.950
You know, of course, I thought it was great, because I had actually seen The Office

601
00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:40.870
years before, when I went to England to do this sitcom seminar-type thing or whatever,

602
00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:45.010
and people were screening the sitcoms, and I was screening the pilot of The Bernie Mac

603
00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:49.990
Show, and Asha Tala, who was a producer on the original Office, was screening the comic

604
00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:53.730
relief episode, I think, of The Office, the one where they wear the red noses, that one.

605
00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:54.190
Yeah.

606
00:22:54.430 --> 00:22:58.150
And I'd never seen The Office before, and it was great, of course, you know, and we were

607
00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:00.170
all, like, congratulating each other afterwards.

608
00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:03.690
And I remember he came to me, he said, Larry, can you promise me one thing?

609
00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:04.150
I said, sure.

610
00:23:04.250 --> 00:23:07.450
He says, please don't let them take this to America and ruin it.

611
00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:08.230
I go, of course not.

612
00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:12.470
I said, by the way, if it ever goes to America, I won't have anything to do with it.

613
00:23:12.730 --> 00:23:13.550
Smash cut.

614
00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.310
I don't work it on the American version of The Office.

615
00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:20.790
And it's funny, in the beginning, this is something, I don't know if you guys, how much

616
00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:27.250
you realize, in the beginning of what was going on, that there was a lot of negativity in showbiz

617
00:23:27.250 --> 00:23:28.050
about our show.

618
00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:34.370
We were kind of sequestered from it, because remember, we were both in mid-L.A., that first

619
00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:35.430
five episodes, you know?

620
00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:35.930
Oh, yeah.

621
00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:38.290
And people thought we were going to ruin The Office.

622
00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:41.310
Like, I would talk to writers around town, and they were real snidey about it.

623
00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:42.290
They said, so what are you working on?

624
00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:43.550
I said, oh, I'm working on The Office.

625
00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:44.290
They go, oh, okay.

626
00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:44.790
I'm like, what?

627
00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:45.790
It's good, guys.

628
00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:46.670
It's going to be good.

629
00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:56.930
You know, so we don't ride ourselves in the corner, especially with Pam and Jim and all

630
00:23:56.930 --> 00:24:02.560
that kind of stuff, and just, you know, just have as much fun as feeling in the writer's

631
00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:05.590
room, because, you know, who cared?

632
00:24:05.650 --> 00:24:06.470
We might be off the air.

633
00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:07.590
Maybe we'll just go for it.

634
00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:10.530
That's such a good insight, because I felt that way, too.

635
00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:15.190
I felt like we were making the show in a bubble, or making it for our own amusement sometimes.

636
00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:17.870
Do you remember our table reads in the beginning?

637
00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:18.890
They weren't around a table.

638
00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:20.370
We would just sit on the set.

639
00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:23.430
We'd all pull up our desk chairs, and we'd sit in a circle.

640
00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:27.950
And then during our hiatus, I did a guest star, and I went in for the table read, and it was

641
00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:29.190
like a big deal.

642
00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:34.190
There was a big table, and everyone sat around it, and you had like a little placard with

643
00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:39.570
your name, and like your lines had been highlighted, and there was network people there.

644
00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:44.830
And I just remember that first season of The Office, we really were just sort of like

645
00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:46.270
on our own, it felt like.

646
00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:46.870
Yeah.

647
00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:48.570
We were the little engine that could.

648
00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:51.510
What was it like in the writer's room?

649
00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:54.770
The way the writer's room went on The Office, especially in the early days, was whatever

650
00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:58.410
idea we had would go in a little index card and wouldn't be slapped on the wall.

651
00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:01.790
You know, after a while, we had all these ideas that were just slapped on the wall that

652
00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:04.690
were just really funny comic ideas of what could happen.

653
00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:09.430
And in many instances, the way we created episodes was we'd put like two or three cards

654
00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:11.270
together, and that would be an episode, you know.

655
00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:15.570
And that's how ideas were generally generated, us just making each other laugh.

656
00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:20.190
And whoever came up with the idea didn't necessarily write the show, by the way.

657
00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:22.050
It was very democratic in that way.

658
00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:23.590
Greg would just hand it out to whoever.

659
00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:29.560
Everybody had a hand in all the episodes, you know, even though we each went out and

660
00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:35.560
wrote a draft, we all contributed to them, you know, and that was a lot of fun.

661
00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:40.000
My one regret was, in the second season, I came up with that Halloween episode idea, and

662
00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:41.220
I really wanted to write that one.

663
00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:45.780
And I was like, nah, you know, I was kind of salty that I didn't get to write it, but

664
00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:46.720
you know, you didn't care.

665
00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:47.340
You got over it.

666
00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:48.000
It was still fun.

667
00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:53.400
But that idea, just from me thinking it would be very funny if Michael had to fire somebody

668
00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:59.080
while they were in costume, like he had to fire somebody during Halloween.

669
00:25:59.200 --> 00:25:59.820
And you're right.

670
00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:00.820
And that's usually how episodes tell.

671
00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:08.140
We so enjoyed your performance in Diversity Day.

672
00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:10.100
I mean, we knew you as a writer.

673
00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:12.220
We knew you as our, you know, our writer, producer.

674
00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:15.920
But then when you got to perform, you were so fantastic.

675
00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:18.980
And then I had just such a new appreciation for it.

676
00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:23.440
I remember thinking you were amazing when we were filming it, but then watching it, you

677
00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:25.740
were so perfect as Mr. Brown.

678
00:26:26.080 --> 00:26:26.900
Oh, thank you, Angela.

679
00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:27.860
That's so nice of you.

680
00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:35.630
Ken, I had the time.

681
00:26:35.870 --> 00:26:37.050
I was kind of going through a trend.

682
00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:47.400
My evil plan had always been to create a show for myself, stand up in an ad, producing

683
00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:50.800
in television, because I felt like Hollywood couldn't find me.

684
00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:55.540
It was better for me to create a space for comedy than I did.

685
00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:56.340
It was just a little different.

686
00:26:56.460 --> 00:27:00.180
And I really enjoyed writing and producing once I started doing it.

687
00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:06.710
And now it may be time to start thinking about this other gear.

688
00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:12.600
And when I was on The Office reading, I think I read that part at the table, but I didn't

689
00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:15.120
have an intention of playing it just because I was real busy.

690
00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:20.500
And I really believe that, you know, we should hire actors, give actors a shot to do this,

691
00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:26.610
even though Greg's philosophy on the show was he really, really fantastic.

692
00:27:26.930 --> 00:27:35.060
And, you know, people like Mindy and BJ and I had no idea Paul was that good of an

693
00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:35.240
actor.

694
00:27:35.380 --> 00:27:36.480
I would have hired him on my show.

695
00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:37.960
You know, he was so great.

696
00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:43.860
And it gave it such an interesting feel, you know, to have writers being a part of it and

697
00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:49.220
to have, you know, people who were in the background, like Angela, some of the ad-libs you guys started

698
00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:50.120
doing in the life.

699
00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:54.560
The way you guys brought those characters to life was not really a lot of material in the

700
00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:55.760
beginning was fantastic.

701
00:27:55.760 --> 00:27:59.680
You know, just even the looks you guys gave and all that stuff really gave this organic

702
00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:00.600
feel to it, you know.

703
00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:02.680
But in the beginning, I really didn't want to play that part.

704
00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:04.540
And Ken kind of talked me into it.

705
00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:06.440
You know, I just wasn't thinking about it.

706
00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:08.380
I just thought, yeah, we should be bringing an actress.

707
00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:11.680
But then I realized that's kind of what we're doing on the show is we're all kind of like

708
00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:13.100
pitching into this thing.

709
00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:23.580
So once I embraced it, I just ended up just having a thing.

710
00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:24.840
It was just so funny.

711
00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:25.960
I was just crying laughing.

712
00:28:25.960 --> 00:28:31.360
Which we probably couldn't even do that episode today, you know, because it was so inappropriate.

713
00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:36.290
Do people approach you and call you Mr. Brown?

714
00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:37.210
All the time.

715
00:28:37.430 --> 00:28:38.050
It's crazy.

716
00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:42.450
People say, like, I have all these different, I guess, identities to people.

717
00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:44.510
Like some people know me from the Nightly Show or the Daily Show.

718
00:28:44.870 --> 00:28:49.690
Some people just know me from the office and from that role, you know, and from other things

719
00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:50.110
that I do.

720
00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:51.050
But it's amazing.

721
00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:52.630
And they don't say Larry Wilmore.

722
00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:53.910
They say Mr. Brown.

723
00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:56.650
And that's it.

724
00:28:57.170 --> 00:28:57.370
Done.

725
00:28:57.370 --> 00:28:58.850
And that's who I am in their minds.

726
00:28:58.850 --> 00:28:59.970
It's not going to change.

727
00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:01.570
But I consider it an honor.

728
00:29:01.750 --> 00:29:07.350
I think how many times, guys, do you get to play a part where I was on screen for what?

729
00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:09.050
A minute and a half, two minutes?

730
00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:12.310
And people remember that years later, the character's name?

731
00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:14.370
I mean, that's an honor to do that, you know?

732
00:29:14.450 --> 00:29:19.830
So the biggest thing people want to know about this episode, Angela, you went through the

733
00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:20.250
questions.

734
00:29:20.570 --> 00:29:22.310
It's about the suggestion box.

735
00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:25.470
They all want to know what was written, what was improvised.

736
00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:30.330
I know I got one line improvised in there that made it in, but they were wondering, like,

737
00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:32.270
what were ones that didn't get read?

738
00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:36.110
Like, you guys must have pitched all kinds of things for that suggestion box.

739
00:29:36.570 --> 00:29:38.110
Not as much as people would think.

740
00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:47.000
I think pretty much my first draft made it through, I think, to the Carson show where his side

741
00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:51.160
was Ed McMahon, and Johnny would do this bit called Karnak, where he would read something

742
00:29:51.160 --> 00:29:52.700
and Ed would repeat it, you know?

743
00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:53.040
Yeah.

744
00:29:53.520 --> 00:30:04.050
He would do this thing, and it would put Dwight in that position where he had to repeat these

745
00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:14.800
things with Michael, and that's kind of how it was, you know, to lead, you know, to the

746
00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:15.920
way that it did, you know?

747
00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:17.400
So I don't remember.

748
00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:31.180
That makes total sense to me.

749
00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:35.520
It makes total sense, and also, if you think about it like that, when you watch it, there

750
00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:39.820
are camera movements that look very deliberate, you know, because you're panning to the people

751
00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:41.260
that have something to say.

752
00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:46.340
The thing that's great about The Office is things that feel, like, by the way, of course

753
00:30:49.060 --> 00:31:06.300
it's great, but the opposite, we also created moments that were not seen because I knew

754
00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:16.260
that.

755
00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:21.440
Well, I think something you were mentioning, that people often don't realize how written

756
00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:24.880
The Office is, that people think it's mostly improvised, which it's not.

757
00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:26.140
It's mostly scripted.

758
00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:29.420
But you guys would even script some of those looks.

759
00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:35.680
You would, in the script, it would say, camera pans to catch Angela looking judgy by her desk.

760
00:31:35.820 --> 00:31:38.140
Like, you started to write in those moments.

761
00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:40.920
Yeah, and it is that combination of things.

762
00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:46.180
It's the combination of us seeing what you guys were doing and then writing towards it.

763
00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:54.370
So it's the, especially, like, that's why those first five episodes are so important,

764
00:31:54.370 --> 00:32:01.040
because all the stuff that, you know, you guys were doing, we noticed all of those early

765
00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:01.420
days.

766
00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:03.300
I think all the writers were in the set.

767
00:32:05.260 --> 00:32:10.520
I think we were all there, you know, watching it, you know, and just learning, you know, from

768
00:32:10.520 --> 00:32:15.980
what you...

769
00:32:15.980 --> 00:32:18.240
It did feel like a real creative collaboration.

770
00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:20.380
I always felt that way, and it's something...

771
00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:21.020
It really was.

772
00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:24.280
